{"episodes":{"156685":"A warm front began to lift north across East Texas and North Louisiana during the afternoon of April 13th, with warm, moist, and unstable air shifting back north across these areas. Meanwhile, a weak upper level disturbance in the zonal flow aloft helped enhance scattered thunderstorm development along and north of the front across portions of East Texas. Given the adequate shear and extent of instability aloft, isolated severe thunderstorms developed and produced reports of large hail across these areas before diminishing during the evening with the loss of heating. However, another more significant shortwave trough traversed the Red River Valley of Southern Oklahoma and North Texas, as the warm front shifted farther north across East Texas to near the Louisiana/Arkansas border. Large scale forcing ahead of this trough and elevated instability aloft resulted in an increase in shower and thunderstorm development during the early morning hours of April 14th, near and ahead of an associated cold front that had begun to enter the region from the northwest. A few isolated severe thunderstorms developed over portions of Northeast Texas, Southwest Arkansas, and North Louisiana, producing instances of large hail before these storms weakened and became more numerous after daybreak, before eventually diminishing from west to east by afternoon.","156903":"Temperatures were below normal as a result of a cold front that had passed through West Texas the day before. Surface winds were easterly which helped to increase low-level moisture. Upper level disturbances passed over the area and combined with elevated instability.  These conditions resulted in a thunderstorm over the northern Permian Basin that produced hail.","157127":"Minor flooding occurred on  the Mississippi River following heavy rainfall in the Mississippi Valley that caused a secondary crest.","157627":"A cold front moving into a very unstable airmass triggered severe thunderstorms.  Some of these storms produced very large hail across portions of West Central Texas.","157636":"An unsettled weather pattern during the week of April 12th generated several days of showers and thunderstorms across North and Central Texas, as multiple upper level disturbances moved across the region. A few of these storms produced large hail.","157747":"Elevated convection developed during the late afternoon and evening well north of a cold front. A few storms produced marginally severe hail.","157842":"A wake low developed on the backside of a cluster of showers and thunderstorms racing south across southeast Louisiana. This drastically enhanced downdraft winds, producing long duration winds around tropical storm strength across the area with peak gusts as strong as a weak hurricane. 13 men perished when these winds capsized a power lift boat.","157843":"A wake low developed on the backside of a cluster of showers and thunderstorms racing south across southeast Louisiana. This drastically enhanced downdraft winds, producing long duration winds around tropical storm strength across the area with peak gusts as strong as a weak hurricane.","157891":"Thunderstorms formed along a frontal boundary in Central Texas and moved through the northeastern part of South Central Texas. Some of these storms produced large hail.","158111":"A cut off upper low slowly worked across the deep south during the middle of April. This produced multiple days of stormy weather."},"events":[{"begin":"2021-04-13T00:02Z","county":"Mason","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2021-04-13T00:02Z","endLat":30.91,"endLon":-98.98,"episode":157627,"id":952892,"injuries":0,"lat":30.91,"location":"0.69 N Pontotoc","lon":-98.98,"magnitude":0.88,"source":"Trained Spotter","state":"TX","type":"Hail","wfo":"SJT"},{"begin":"2021-04-13T00:05Z","county":"Llano","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2021-04-13T00:05Z","endLat":30.85,"endLon":-98.93,"episode":157891,